<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malaika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a> (according to the account crediting Adam Salim); first recorded 1959 or 1960</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Malaika&quot; is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> love song, possibly the most famous in Tanzania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and one of the most widely known Swahili songs in the world. Malaika means &quot;angel&quot; in Swahili, a word long used for a beautiful girl. The title varies from version to version, as &quot;Malaika Nakupenda Malaika&quot;, &quot;Ewe Malaika&quot; or &quot;My Angel&quot;, and so do the words, whose verses are often rearranged, left out or combined.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The authorship of Malaika is disputed. Most people credit Adam Salim, a little-published Tanzanian songwriter born in 1916, who is said to have composed it while living in Nairobi in 1945 and 1946 for his girlfriend Halima Ramadhani Maruwa. According to this story their parents disapproved of the match, and Halima was forced to marry a wealthy Asian man, a tajir.</p><p>The Kenyan singer Fadhili William, who made the first recording with his band The Jambo Boys, always insisted that he wrote the song and described the circumstances in detail, but he is recognised as composer only for royalty purposes. The producer Charles Worrod credited Grant Charo, William's brother-in-law, who is not known to have confirmed the claim, and Lucas Tututu of Mombasa also claimed to be the author.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song is sung by a poor young man who wishes to marry his beloved, his angel or little bird, but is defeated by the bride price. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> is probably Tanzanian, possibly Kenyan. Fadhili William's early recording has only two verses; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miriam-makeba">Miriam Makeba</a>'s adds a third, the Pesa verse, which also appears on a later record by William. Makeba's and Angélique Kidjo's versions change the song considerably.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Worrod re-recorded the song at Equator Sound Studios and marketed it until it won an international audience, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miriam-makeba">Miriam Makeba</a>'s early recording carried it throughout the continent and eventually the world. Through her performances it reached Harry Belafonte, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>, Usha Uthup and others, and it is a staple of many African musicians.</p><p>The Swedish group the Hep Stars heard it from a chef at their hotel in Nairobi while filming there and recorded it at Europafilm Studios in April 1967. Their single entered Kvällstoppen at number 4 on 9 May 1967, held number 1 for five weeks and stayed 18 weeks on the chart, the only song wholly in an African language to reach number one on a Swedish chart. Boney M. released it in 1981 from their album Boonoonoonoos; it reached number 13 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the top ten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and a Lambada remix followed in 1989.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malaika” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
